/*
  Localizable.strings (English, base/development language)

  Scaffolding, not a full localization pass — this app has on the order of
  a thousand-plus user-facing strings across ~65 files, most of them
  dynamically formatted with live financial data (percentages, prices,
  plain-language narrative sentences built from scored components) rather
  than simple static labels. Wrapping all of them in String(localized:)
  and translating them is real, substantial future work, correctly out of
  scope for a single pass without a compiler available to verify each
  change across that many call sites.

  What this file and the few call sites wired to it (RootView.Tab.label,
  as of this pass) demonstrate is the mechanism: project.yml already sets
  SWIFT_EMIT_LOC_STRINGS: YES on the main target, and this Resources/
  folder is already in the target's resources path — so any future
  String(localized:) call automatically gets picked up by Xcode's string
  extraction, and any future .lproj directory added here is picked up as
  a new supported language with no other project changes needed. The next
  step, when this becomes a priority, is working section by section
  through the UI/ directory converting static (non-interpolated) labels
  first, then deciding a strategy for the many dynamically-formatted
  strings (which need String(format:) call sites converted to
  locale-aware formatting — NumberFormatter/ FormatStyle — not just a
  strings-table entry).
*/

